  "id": "COMPOUND:28278 - PROTEIN:585",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Npc172494 may interact with Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase through multiple pathways, with the latter being a common target in several of the identified relationships. The first path links Npc172494 to Tumor necrosis factor receptor superfamily member 10B, which in turn is targeted by a compound that directly acts on Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. The second and third paths connect Npc172494 to Prostaglandin E2 receptor EP4 subtype, which is targeted by Anhydrofurospongin-1 and DL-dimyristoylphosphatidylglycerol, both of which also target Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. These findings indicate that Npc172494 may exert its effects through a shared mechanism involving the regulation of Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, possibly through the modulation of signaling pathways that are downstream of the receptor. The integration of these pathways suggests that Npc172494 may act as a modulator of Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase activity, potentially through the activation or inhibition of downstream targets. The evidence supports a role for Npc172494 in influencing the activity of Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
